      "narrative": "The air in the Copper Finch hangs thick with the smell of damp wool and old ale. Mara Venn leans against the bar, her charcoal coat pulled tight, a half-empty cup of tea cradled in her gloved hands. When you approach and ask about the Ash Gate, she gives you a long, appraising look.\n\nShe sets her cup down with a soft click.\n\n\"The Ash Gate.\" Her voice is low, careful. \"That's a question that gets people looking at you sideways in this town.\"\n\nShe studies you a moment longer, then shakes her head slowly. \"I've looked into it, sure. Talked to the old ferryman, Bram, before he...\" She pauses, a shadow passing over her face. \"Before he died. But the records are a mess. Half the documents in the archive are water-damaged, the other half read like someone wrote them in a panic. Every time I think I've found a thread, it leads to a blank page or a contradiction.\"\n\nShe shrugs, the gesture carrying more frustration than indifference. \"The gate's been closed longer than anyone living remembers clearly. What I *do* know doesn't paint a pretty picture, but it's fragments. Nothing solid enough to act on.\" Her eyes narrow slightly. \"Why the sudden interest?\""
    },
