      "narrative": "Mara Venn's jaw tightens. She picks up her tea, takes a slow sip, and sets it down again. The hesitation tells you she's weighing something.\n\n\"Old Bram,\" she says finally. \"Bram was the ferryman. He ran the skiff across the channel, and he held the only key to the Ash Gate that ever seemed to work. If someone wanted passage through\u2014legitimate passage, the kind that didn't involve climbing or breaking things\u2014they went to Bram.\"\n\nShe glances toward the shuttered window, toward where the gate stands beyond the rain-streaked glass. \"He kept a ledger. Names, dates, cargo. I saw it once, years ago. Thick as your fist, leather-bound, pages yellowed.\" Her eyes meet yours again. \"He kept it in his shack, down by the water. Before the fire took it.\"\n\nShe lets that land. \"The shack burned three nights before Bram died. Everything inside turned to ash and mud.\""
