      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door swings shut behind you, cutting off the low murmur of voices and the clink of cups. The morning air hits you\u2014damp, cool, carrying the faint mineral tang of wet stone and the sharper note of woodsmoke from somewhere up the street.\n\nThe road leading to the Ash Gate is cobbled but uneven, the stones worn smooth by generations of feet. It curves past a row of shuttered houses, their windows dark, and then opens onto a stretch of gravel that slopes gently downhill. The gate comes into view slowly, as if reluctant to be seen all at once.\n\nIt rises from the ground like a broken tooth\u2014a cracked black arch standing alone against a grey sky. No wall flanks it, no fence extends from either side. Just the arch, dark stone veined with pale lichen, the iron portcullis within lowered and rusted into place. Beyond it, through the bars, you can see only a stretch of muddy ground and the grey smear of the channel beyond.\n\nA figure sits on a moss-eaten stone block to the left of the gate, wrapped in a travel-stained cloak. They look up as you approach\u2014a courier's leather satchel slung across their chest, a smear of road dirt on their cheek."
    },
